A||||3||thomas_l||5-sp trans mystery revealed!||||8||
Z||000000||thomas_l||04-16-2001||09:28 AM||||As I mentioned in another thread, I had a transmission weekend and wanted to share the outcome and post some questions. My 87 GT came without any trans so I did not have the benefit of taking things apart in the first place. After getting the trans mounted, I had to rig the shift cable with an extension on the trans lever and change it from a ball mount to a pin mount. The select cable seemed OK. I couldn't find anywhere to hang the slave cyl! Off to pull-a-part I went and found some brackets (here is clue #1) off an 84 4-speed. To make a long story short, I had to fabricate the slave bracket and rebuild the slave cyl that was on the junkyard part. After wrapping up the job I (clue #2) couldn't find the reverse light switch. I then started it up and went into "first" and rolled backward! Seems the junkyard shipped me a 4 speed. OK - now I don't feel so bad about having to rig up the clutch and shift cables  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">  Anyway, I plan to autocross this car so I really don't care if it has 5 or 4 gears, I just want it to go into 1 and 2 really good. I would like to see if anyone can specify the correct items to make the shifter work better. As far as I can tell, I have the 5 speed shifter and cables that used to go to the Getrag 5. I can shift OK, but the reach on the shift cable isn't confidence inspiring, causing an occaisional grind going into 3rd. I believe that I could get away with a 6 cyl, 4 speed shift cable. The select cable seems OK and the shifter works OK. I might go to a 4 speed shifter some day to get reverse lights. About the only thing I plan to add to my engineering is some heat insulation around the clutch slave as it is only a few inches off the downpipe and has no shield.<P>Besides the transmission fun, the car runs out pretty good. It badly needs alignment and wheel balance - it will almost change lanes when you stomp the gas. I took it on a 120 mi round trip yesterday afternoon and made it back home w/o a tow truck.<P>- Thomas.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://fearlessdata.com/fiero/87GT_small.jpg">  ||||reg||8
Z||000001||StuGood||04-16-2001||10:00 AM||||I admire your patience and persistence, getting the car running despite all of the challenges (getting the wrong trans, having to fabricate brackets, etc.).  Sounds like it's been a learning experience!<P>Probably doesn't matter at this point, but my favorite method to "positively identify (?)" a Getrag, is to bring a magnet with (I normally carry a little telescoping magnet in my pocket).  The Getrag has a steel (magnetic) cover plate on one end (the end away from the engine side), while the rest of the case is aluminum (nonmagnetic).<P>You can quickly tell if it's a Getrag, even if the trans is installed in a car.     ||||reg||6
Z||000002||thomas_l||04-16-2001||03:15 PM||||I wasn't really having a problem identifying a Getrag - for the price I paid, I really wasn't expecting one. What threw me was that I got a 4 speed and really wasn't expecting it. All the clues were there but I had my mind on other things (getting the thing running!). I was hoping that someone who has deliberately put a 4 speed in a V6 model could say what items they had to swap/fabricate...then maybe I can improve on my handiwork.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||04-16-2001||10:13 PM||||Thomas,<BR>Good luck with your project.<BR>Forgive me if you knew this already, but 85 and early 86 V-6s came with a 4-speed. You should be able to do the entire conversion with bolt-in parts. I don't know all the particulars, but I'm sure others have been there.  <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
